%I A398007 #25 Aug 25 2026 00:30:10
%S A398007 2,4,5,8,9
%N A398007 a(n) is the largest k such that the vertices of a k X k grid can be painted in n colors without having any 3 collinear vertices of the same color.
%C A398007 User Oray from Puzzling StackExchange proved that a(5) is 9 (see links).
%C A398007 a(n) <= 2*n. This is because a row of size 2*n + 1 painted in n colors will always have at least 3 vertices of the same color.
%H A398007 Dmitry Kamenetsky, <a href="/A398007/a398007.txt">Best known solutions and lower bounds for n <= 10</a>.
%H A398007 Dmitry Kamenetsky, <a href="https://puzzling.stackexchange.com/questions/139132/9x9-grid-with-5-colours-with-no-three-collinear-cells-of-the-same-colour">9x9 grid with 5 colours with no three collinear cells of the same colour</a>, Puzzling StackExchange.
%H A398007 Michał Zapała, <a href="https://puzzling.stackexchange.com/questions/138583/can-an-8-%C3%97-8-grid-be-colored-with-4-colors-so-that-no-three-colinear-points-are">Can an 8 X 8 grid be colored with 4 colors so that no three colinear points are of the same color?</a>, Puzzling StackExchange.
%e A398007 a(3) = 5, since a 5 X 5 grid is the largest grid with 3 colors without any 3 collinear vertices of the same color:
%e A398007   21010
%e A398007   12120
%e A398007   00121
%e A398007   01202
%e A398007   20201
%K A398007 nonn,more,new
%O A398007 1,1
